// REJECTED - item 378: a component config field whose declared type is a
// transparent alias of an ARROW type.
//
// A config value is injected as static data at plug/spawn/load time, so a
// config field's declared type must be built, transitively, out of data. An
// arrow field is a live callable the body can invoke past every authority
// fold: no ticket, no reach attribution, no `emission[...]` bound to exceed.
//
// The alias is the point. `_resolve_type_aliases` substitutes `Handler` away
// before the config walk runs, so the checker judges `(Str) -> Str` and names
// the erased spelling rather than the alias the author wrote. Writing the
// arrow through an alias is not a way around the rule.
//
// Everything else here is accepted: swap `Handler` for `Str` and the file
// compiles, so the only refusal this fixture carries is the config one.
//
// This is also the fixture the formal harness grew its config-is-data row for
// (issue 1161). The model's two other G4 rules both judge a CROSSING -
// `Oracle.g4OK` the emission marker, `Oracle.hostAcquireOK` the acquire
// bracket - and there is no crossing here to judge: the offence is in a
// DECLARED TYPE, which is a fact of a kind the export did not carry. Until it
// did, this refusal read as `missed-G4` wherever the file was put.
//
// expected error:
//   config field `on_row` of component `Loader` has type `(Str) -> Str`,
//   which reaches an arrow (function) type; a config field must be static data

type Handler = (Str) -> Str

service Sink {
  emission[log] fn write(row: Str) -> Int
}

component Loader requires log: Sink provides out: Sink {
  config { source: Str, on_row: Handler }

  provide out {
    fn write(row) {
      emit log.write(row)
      return 1
    }
  }
}
